-- title:
--   Inertia-invariant torsion of J_H(M) bounded by finite part
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a nonzero $M$ with $p \mid M$ but $p^2 \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit whose image under the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is $1$; assume the $q$-series `jqModC ℚ` lies in the level-one $q$-expansion function field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, and let $\mathfrak{X}$ be an integral model datum `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` for $X_H(M)$ over the base ring $R\,p$, whose geometric curve model $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}$ has function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Let $\theta_0$ be a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism of that function field which, on elements coming from level $M/p$, acts on Laurent series by the substitution $q \mapsto q^p$ (`qExpand`), and assume $\theta_0$ implements $\mathfrak{X}.w$ on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points: whenever two sections $y,y'$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.\mathrm{toBase}$ satisfy $y'$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$, the first projection and $\mathfrak{X}.w.\mathrm{hom}$ equals $y$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$ and the first projection, the associated places satisfy $\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,y' = \mathrm{ofAlgAut}(\theta_0) \cdot \mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,y$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit of $A$, with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$, let $\Lambda$ be level data and $O$ a Néron object `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM A hA Λ`, so that $O.G \to \mathrm{base}\,p$ carries a relative group law and $O.\mathrm{pts}$ identifies $J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}, \mathtt{xHFunctionFieldBar}\,M\,H)$ with the sections over the generic point. The hypotheses further require: that the $\mathrm{Pic}^0$-designation $(O.G, O.g)$ with zero section the unit of $O.L$ represents the subfunctor of rigidified line bundles on $\mathfrak{X}$ that are fibrewise algebraically trivial, rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_\infty$, both over $R\,p$ ($hD$) and after base change to $\mathbb{Q}$ ($hDQ$), the latter base change being separated; an Abel–Jacobi morphism $\mathrm{ajQ}$ over the $\mathbb{Q}$-fibre which sends the $\varepsilon_\infty$-section to the zero section and for which the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along any point $x$ over a field is the line bundle of the divisor $(x)$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of $\varepsilon_\infty$; compatibility of the two Poincaré bundles; a comparison morphism $\mathrm{kQ}$ between the fibres over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and over $\mathbb{Q}$ commuting with both projections; a morphism $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ from the geometric curve to $O.G$ obtained by composing $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$, $\mathrm{kQ}$, $\mathrm{ajQ}$ and the first projection, lying over the generic point, and a geometric base point $\overline{\varepsilon}$ mapping to $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_\infty$ and sent by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ to the unit section; additivity of $O.\mathrm{pts}$ for the relative group law supplied by $hD$; and the pinning condition that for all geometric points $x$ and $s$ of the curve model with $s$ the $\varepsilon_\infty$-point, the degree-zero divisor $(\mathrm{place}\,x) - (\mathrm{place}\,s)$ has $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of its class equal to $x$ followed by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$. The conclusion: there exists $C \in \mathbb{N}$ with $C > 0$ such that for every $n > 0$, the number of classes $x \in J_H(M)$ that are $n$-torsion in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ and fixed by every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ is at most $\#(O.\mathrm{finPts}\,n) \cdot C$, where $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,n$ is the subgroup generated by those $n$-torsion classes whose section extends to the place $\Lambda.\sigma_A$.
--
--   This is the comparison, at a place $A$ above a prime $p$ exactly dividing the level, between the inertia-invariant $n$-torsion of $J_H(M)$ and the part of the torsion extending over the Néron object, with a bound uniform in $n$ (the intended constant being the order of the component group). It is used in the analysis of the toric part of the reduction at $p$ and in the study of the inertia action on the Tate module of $J_H(M)$, which feed the level-lowering step at $p \parallel M$.
